### What is the N+1 query problem?

The N+1 problem occurs when you load a parent entity that has a LAZY collection. Query 1 loads N parents. Then, the first time you access each parent's collection, Hibernate fires one query per parent to load that collection. The total is 1 + N queries.

At National Bank of India, Rajesh loads 100 accounts. Each account has a list of trades. Without any optimization, Hibernate fires 101 queries.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class AccountReportService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountReportService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public void generateReport() {
        List<Account> accounts = accountRepo.findAll();
        // Query 1: SELECT * FROM accounts (loads 100 accounts)

        for (Account account : accounts) {
            account.getTrades().size();
            // Query 2: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
            // Query 3: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id = 'SAV2002'
            // ... Query 101: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id = 'SAV2100'
        }
        // Total: 1 + 100 = 101 queries!
    }
}
```

### How do you detect N+1 queries?

The simplest detection method is enabling SQL logging and counting queries per request. You can also enable Hibernate statistics for a detailed query count.

```yaml
# application.yml: enable SQL logging
spring:
  jpa:
    show-sql: true
    properties:
      hibernate:
        format_sql: true
        generate_statistics: true
# Hibernate statistics log: "Session Metrics { 101 queries executed }"
```

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.hibernate.SessionFactory;
import org.hibernate.stat.Statistics;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManagerFactory;

public class QueryCounter {

    public void countQueries(EntityManagerFactory emf) {
        SessionFactory sf = emf.unwrap(SessionFactory.class);
        Statistics stats = sf.getStatistics();
        stats.setStatisticsEnabled(true);

        long before = stats.getQueryExecutionCount();
        // ... run your service method ...
        long after = stats.getQueryExecutionCount();

        System.out.println("Queries executed: " + (after - before));
        // Output: Queries executed: 101  (N+1 detected!)
    }
}
```

### How does JOIN FETCH fix N+1?

JOIN FETCH loads the parent and its collection in one SQL query using a JOIN. Instead of 101 queries, you get exactly 1 query.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a JOIN FETCH a.trades")
    List<Account> findAllWithTrades();
    // Generates ONE query:
    // SELECT a.*, t.* FROM accounts a
    // INNER JOIN trades t ON a.account_id = t.account_id
    // Result: 1 query instead of 101!
}
```

```text
Expected SQL output:
SELECT a.account_id, a.holder_name, a.balance,
       t.trade_id, t.symbol, t.amount
FROM accounts a
INNER JOIN trades t ON a.account_id = t.account_id
-- 1 query, all data loaded
```

### How does @EntityGraph fix N+1?

`@EntityGraph` is a declarative fetch plan. You annotate the repository method and Hibernate builds the JOIN automatically. No JPQL needed.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.EntityGraph;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"trades"})
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a")
    List<Account> findAllWithTradesGraph();
    // Same result as JOIN FETCH but declarative.
    // Generates: SELECT a.*, t.* FROM accounts a
    //            LEFT JOIN trades t ON a.account_id = t.account_id

    // EntityGraph with multiple attributes:
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"trades", "nominees"})
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.accountId = :id")
    Account findWithTradesAndNominees(String id);
}
```

### How does @BatchSize fix N+1?

`@BatchSize` does not eliminate lazy loading. Instead, it batches the lazy loads using an IN clause. Instead of 100 separate queries, Hibernate fires 100/batchSize queries.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import org.hibernate.annotations.BatchSize;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    private String holderName;

    @Column(precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal balance;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @BatchSize(size = 50)
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
    // Without @BatchSize: 100 accounts = 100 queries for trades
    // With @BatchSize(50): 100 accounts = 2 batch queries (50 + 50)
    // Batch query: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id IN (?, ?, ... x50)
}
```

```text
Without @BatchSize (100 accounts):
  Query 1: SELECT * FROM accounts                           -- 1 query
  Query 2: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001' -- per account
  ... 100 more queries
  Total: 101 queries

With @BatchSize(size = 50):
  Query 1: SELECT * FROM accounts                           -- 1 query
  Query 2: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id IN (SAV2001..SAV2050) -- batch 1
  Query 3: SELECT * FROM trades WHERE account_id IN (SAV2051..SAV2100) -- batch 2
  Total: 3 queries!
```

### How is JOIN FETCH different from @EntityGraph and @BatchSize?

All three fix N+1, but they work differently and have different trade-offs.

| Feature | JOIN FETCH | @EntityGraph | @BatchSize |
|---------|-----------|-------------|------------|
| Query count | 1 query | 1 query | N/batch + 1 |
| Approach | Explicit JPQL | Declarative annotation | Batched lazy load |
| Pagination | Breaks `Pageable` | Breaks `Pageable` | Works with `Pageable` |
| Multiple collections | MultipleBagFetchException | MultipleBagFetchException | Works fine |
| Memory | All data in one result set | All data in one result set | Loads in batches |
| Use case | Known fetch pattern | Same, cleaner syntax | Default for all lazy loads |

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Pageable;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    // JOIN FETCH: breaks pagination (Hibernate warns "firstResult/maxResults with collection fetch")
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a JOIN FETCH a.trades")
    List<Account> findAllWithTrades();  // returns List, not Page

    // @BatchSize: works with pagination (lazy loads fire in batches after page loads)
    Page<Account> findAll(Pageable pageable);
    // Trades loaded lazily in batches of 50 when accessed
}
```

### What is MultipleBagFetchException?

When you JOIN FETCH two `List` collections in one query, Hibernate creates a Cartesian product. For 100 accounts x 10 trades x 3 nominees = 3,000 rows. Hibernate treats an unordered `List` as a "bag" and throws `MultipleBagFetchException`.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    // WRONG: JOIN FETCH on two Lists throws MultipleBagFetchException
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a JOIN FETCH a.trades JOIN FETCH a.nominees")
    List<Account> findWithTradesAndNominees();
    // org.hibernate.loader.MultipleBagFetchException:
    // cannot simultaneously fetch multiple bags
}
```

Three ways to fix this:

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import org.hibernate.annotations.BatchSize;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id
    private String accountId;

    // FIX 1: Change one collection from List to Set
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account")
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();  // keep as List

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account")
    private Set<Nominee> nominees = new HashSet<>();  // change to Set
    // Now JOIN FETCH on both works (Set is not a "bag")

    // FIX 2: Use @BatchSize on the second collection
    // @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account")
    // @BatchSize(size = 50)
    // private List<Nominee> nominees = new ArrayList<>();
    // JOIN FETCH only trades; nominees load lazily in batches

    // FIX 3: Fetch one collection per query (in service layer)
}
```

### What are common mistakes with N+1?

The most common mistake is not detecting N+1 at all. The second most common mistake is applying JOIN FETCH everywhere without considering the Cartesian product.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class AccountMistakeDemo {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountMistakeDemo(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // WRONG: using findAll() and then accessing lazy collections
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public void wrongApproach() {
        List<Account> accounts = accountRepo.findAll();
        for (Account a : accounts) {
            System.out.println(a.getTrades().size());  // N+1!
        }
    }

    // CORRECT: use JOIN FETCH or @EntityGraph
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public void correctApproach() {
        List<Account> accounts = accountRepo.findAllWithTrades();
        for (Account a : accounts) {
            System.out.println(a.getTrades().size());  // already loaded, no extra queries
        }
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know about N+1?

A senior engineer should set `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size=25` as a global safety net. This applies `@BatchSize` to all lazy collections application-wide, preventing the worst N+1 scenarios without changing any entity code.

```yaml
# application.yml: global safety net for N+1
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        default_batch_fetch_size: 25
        # All lazy collections now load in batches of 25
        # 100 accounts: 1 + 4 batch queries instead of 1 + 100
```

```java
package com.bank.config;

// Senior-level N+1 prevention checklist:
// 1. Set default_batch_fetch_size = 25 globally
// 2. Override @ManyToOne to LAZY (default is EAGER)
// 3. Use JOIN FETCH for known access patterns
// 4. Use @EntityGraph for repository methods
// 5. Disable OSIV: spring.jpa.open-in-view=false
// 6. Use DTO projections for read-only API responses
// 7. Enable Hibernate statistics in dev/staging
// 8. Add query count assertions in integration tests
```
